 ],
 "literal": "Sowing mountain rice in the expanse of waterfalls, / weeding green bowstring hemp along with large-leaved wild jasmine, / in the small settlement with a fence of Malabar glory lilies, if hungry, / they eat by bartering the tusks of fierce-eyed elephants. / On the western slopes of Kolli hills belonging to Ori of the strong bow, / like the statue there, she is of gentle innocence, / she who has bamboo-like large shoulders that are difficult to embrace.",
 "translation": "In the small hamlet fenced with glory lilies, where mountain rice is sown in the expanse of waterfalls, and green bowstring hemp is weeded along with large-leaved wild jasmine, the people, when hungry, survive by bartering the tusks of fierce-eyed elephants. She, who has large, bamboo-like shoulders so difficult to embrace, is as gentle and innocent as the statue on the western slopes of the Kolli hills belonging to Ori of the strong bow.",
 "modern": "அருவிப் பரப்பில் மலைநெல்லை விதைத்து, பெரிய இலைகளையுடைய காட்டு மல்லிகையோடு பசுமையான மரல் செடிகளையும் களையும், காந்தள் மலர்களை வேலியாகக் கொண்ட சிறுகுடியில் வாழ்பவர்கள், பசி எடுத்தால், சினங்கொண்ட கண்ணையுடைய யானையின் தந்தங்களை விற்று உண்பார்கள். வலிய வில்லையுடைய ஓரி என்பவனுக்குரிய கொல்லி மலையின் மேற்குச் சரிவில் உள்ள பாவை போன்ற மென்மையான இயல்புடையவள் அவள்; தழுவுவதற்கு அரிய மூங்கில் போன்ற பெரிய தோள்களை உடையவள் அவள்.",
 "explanation": "This poem is spoken by the hero to himself, expressing his deep desire and the difficulty of attaining the heroine. He describes her mountain home in the Kuriñji landscape, where the inhabitants sow mountain rice and weed wild jasmine. When hungry, they do not suffer in poverty but barter precious elephant tusks for food, showing the wealth and pride of her clan. He compares the heroine's beauty and innocence to the famous, exquisite statue (Kolli Pāvai) on the western slopes of the Kolli hills ruled by the generous chieftain Valvil Ōri. The difficult terrain and the preciousness of the tusks mirror the difficulty of attaining the heroine, whose bamboo-like shoulders are described as hard to embrace.",
